WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.082
[Music begins] [John Spouge, MD, PhD]&nbsp;
I have a background in &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.082 --> 00:00:05.340
mathematics, a PhD in fact, and an MD.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:12.000
And I spent a lot of the early part of the 80s&nbsp;
trying to combine the two backgrounds. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.655
when NCBI was created in 1989, it&nbsp;
was an amazing opportunity for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.655 --> 00:00:21.600
because here was recognition of&nbsp;
both backgrounds simultaneously.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.920
I certainly had an opportunity&nbsp;
to live my life as a physician,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:32.000
but I felt that I could contribute by actually&nbsp;
moving society forward with the research.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:38.070
The research that I'm doing falls into a&nbsp;
couple of distinct categories - virology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:45.480
and taxonomy. The interest in virology made&nbsp;
it relatively easy for me to study COVID.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.980
And so I actually became very interested&nbsp;
in the initial phase of the epidemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:55.428
and what it could tell you about the&nbsp;
basic reproduction number "R-NAUGHT",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.428 --> 00:00:58.680
which is the basis of Public Health policy.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:07.080
One of my more recent papers was to estimate&nbsp;
the basic reproduction number for COVID in 152&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:13.260
countries. It was an unusual feat because&nbsp;
it required regularizing a lot of data.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.713
One of the most important areas that I have&nbsp;
worked on is definitely taxonomy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.713 --> 00:01:24.780
The basic idea is dividing the genomes of&nbsp;
different species up into short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.442
segments which you then compare to&nbsp;
a database to identify the species.

00:01:29.442 --> 00:01:37.713
I am very proud of the fact that I was involved&nbsp;
in the conferences on plants and fungi - for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.713 --> 00:01:46.140
selecting the genes for identifying species&nbsp;
in those organisms called DNA barcoding. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:52.020
barcoding was established in animals but&nbsp;
not in plants or fungi. Both conferences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:57.660
for barcoding needed someone to crunch the data&nbsp;
and I really was very glad that I could do that.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:04.440
I think the future of NLM lies in&nbsp;
bringing a lot of the data analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:10.860
that we do as research now, directly into&nbsp;
a hospital. To be able to set up clinical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:17.340
trials relatively easily, to be able to&nbsp;
collect data in standardized formats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:24.939
and all of these things would make&nbsp;
prediction and diagnosis much easier.

00:02:24.939 --> 00:02:28.873
[Music]

00:02:28.873 --> 00:02:30.973
[Music fades to silence]

